{
  "version": 1,
  "_overlayComment": "Most concepts partition their domain's steps: contiguous, one rung each, and a problem belongs to exactly one of them by its step. A concept with `requires` is an OVERLAY instead - it claims problems by what they ARE rather than by how hard they are, and it is tried FIRST. This exists because difficulty and shape are different axes: `5 + ? = 8` is exactly as hard as `5 + 3 = 8` and earns the same rung, but it is not the same idea and must not get the same lesson. Overlays are additive: they never claim a step, so the contiguity guarantee below is untouched. The same mechanism is what `requiresCarry` (populated on 995 problems, inexpressible as a step range) will use when carrying gets its own lesson.",
  "_comment": "The concept ladder: how curriculum steps group into TEACHABLE IDEAS. A step is a difficulty rung; a concept is the thing a child has to understand to climb it. Ranges must be contiguous per domain from step 0 - tools/validate_math_concepts.mjs fails the build otherwise, and reports every step inside a range that has no authored problems. Runtime lookup lives in godot/scripts/math/concept_ladder.gd; the tutorial each concept opens with lives in data/tutorials/math_tutorials.json.",
  "_gapsComment": "Every rung of the ladder with no authored problems on it, declared. tools/validate_math_concepts.mjs fails the build on an UNDECLARED hole, and equally on a declared one that has since been filled - so a gap can only be closed by deleting its entry here, and a new one can never appear quietly. `thin` is the same discipline one notch softer: a rung with fewer than `minPerStep` problems, which the ladder can still promote onto but cannot give a child three different questions on.",
  "_narrowComment": "How WIDE a rung is, as opposed to how many problems sit on it. `thin` counts problems; `narrow` counts distinct facts, because twenty problems drawn from two facts is a rung a child can pass by remembering two answers, and the problem count cannot see the difference. More framings of the same fact raise the count and never the width, which is exactly why this has to be measured separately before any framing set is widened. Same discipline as knownGaps and knownThin: an undeclared narrow rung fails the build, and so does a declaration for a rung that has since widened. A fact is the multiset of numbers in play -- what the prompt writes plus what it asks for -- so \"11 + 3\", \"3 + 11\" and \"11 + ? = 14\" are ONE fact, which is also how deriveCurriculumStep already treats them. tools/validate_math_concepts.mjs documents the proxy.",
  "gapPolicy": {
    "minPerStep": 6,
    "minFactsPerStep": 4
  },
  "knownGaps": [],
  "_unreachableComment": "Concepts with authored problems that the owl can never serve, each with a reason. EMPTY as shipped: the blanket operand rail was removed by the owner's grade-4 decision (2026-08) — the step ladder paces each child instead — so every authored concept is reachable. tools/validate_math_concepts.mjs re-populates this obligation automatically if a numeric rail ever returns.",
  "knownUnreachable": [],
  "knownThin": [],
  "knownNarrow": [
    {
      "domain": "subtraction",
      "steps": [
        5
      ],
      "why": "One number triple, and one is the ceiling. Step 5 is a maximum operand of ten with no borrow, and a borrow needs the minuend's ones digit to be smaller than the subtrahend's -- ten ends in zero, so nothing can be smaller than it. That leaves 10-0 and 10-10, which are the two halves of the single bond 0 + 10 = 10."
    },
    {
      "domain": "subtraction",
      "steps": [
        15
      ],
      "why": "Two triples. 20-0, 20-10 and 20-20 are the only subtractions inside twenty that derive onto step 15, and they carry {0,20,20} and {10,10,20} between them. Structurally narrow rather than under-authored: this rung reached the problem-count floor in 2026-08 and its width did not move, which is the whole reason this list exists."
    },
    {
      "domain": "multiplication",
      "steps": [
        9
      ],
      "why": "Step 9 is the seven-times table, and a fact belongs to the EARLIEST table that contains it. Once every 7x fact owned by an earlier table or by the squares is taken out, 7x8 and 7x9 are all that is left."
    },
    {
      "domain": "multiplication",
      "steps": [
        10
      ],
      "why": "Step 10 is the eight-times table under the same earliest-table rule. 8x8 is a square (step 7) and every other 8x fact belongs to an earlier table, so 8x9 is the only one left -- which is itself the pedagogical point: by the time a child reaches it there is almost nothing new in the table."
    },
    {
      "domain": "division",
      "steps": [
        10
      ],
      "why": "A division fact sits one step after the multiplication fact it inverts, so step 10 inverts multiplication step 9 and inherits its two facts: 56 and 63, each divisible two ways."
    },
    {
      "domain": "division",
      "steps": [
        11
      ],
      "why": "Inverts multiplication step 10, which has one fact. 72/8 and 72/9 are the two halves of 8x9 and therefore one triple."
    },
    {
      "domain": "addition",
      "steps": [
        0,
        1
      ],
      "why": "The bottom of the ladder, and §1 says so in as many words: \"addition step 0 is the four facts inside 0+0..1+1 and no authoring can add a fifth\". Three triples each -- {0,0,0}, {0,1,1}, {1,1,2} on step 0, and {0,2,2}, {1,2,3}, {2,2,4} on step 1, since 1+0 is the same triple as 0+1. This is exactly the rung where §5 puts the floor on PRESENTATION instead: twelve marker shapes and every short framing, because the arithmetic cannot be widened and the way it looks can."
    },
    {
      "domain": "multiplication",
      "steps": [
        8
      ],
      "why": "The six-times table under the earliest-table rule. Every 6x fact belonging to an earlier table is that table's, and 6x6 is a square, which leaves 6x7, 6x8 and 6x9 -- the three the classroom data calls the hardest single facts, so the rung is narrow and heavy at once."
    },
    {
      "domain": "division",
      "steps": [
        9
      ],
      "why": "Inverts multiplication step 8 and inherits its three facts: 42, 48 and 54, each divisible two ways."
    }
  ],
